[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m We need to find the divisors of \(9!\) that have a units digit of 1. These divisors must satisfy the condition \(k \equiv 1 \mod 10\). Such numbers are co-prime to 10 (i.e., not divisible by 2 or 5) and congruent to 1 modulo 10.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m The co-prime to 10 divisors of \(9!\) are the divisors of \(3^4 \times 7\). We need to find numbers of the form \(3^a \times 7^b\) that are congruent to 1 modulo 10.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m We check the possible values of \(a\) and \(b\):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m - For \(b = 0\), \(3^a \equiv 1 \mod 10\). This happens when \(a \equiv 0 \mod 4\), so \(a = 0\) or \(a = 4\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m - For \(b = 1\), \(3^a \times 7 \equiv 1 \mod 10\) implies \(3^a \equiv 3 \mod 10\), so \(a = 1\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m Thus, the valid pairs \((a, b)\) are:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m - \((0, 0)\): \(3^0 \times 7^0 = 1\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m - \((4, 0)\): \(3^4 \times 7^0 = 81\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m - \((1, 1)\): \(3^1 \times 7^1 = 21\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m These divisors are 1, 21, and 81. Summing these:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m \[ 1 + 21 + 81 = 103 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m 
