[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m First, we factorize \(9!\):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m \[9! = 362880 = 2^7 \times 3^4 \times 5 \times 7\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m We need to find the divisors of \(9!\) that have units digit 1, which implies they are congruent to 1 modulo 10. These divisors must also be congruent to 1 modulo 10. 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m Given that the number must be odd (to have units digit 1), it must not include the factor 5. Therefore, we consider the divisors of the form \(3^b \times 7^d\) where \(0 \leq b \leq 4\) and \(0 \leq d \leq 1\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m We need \(3^b \times 7^d \equiv 1 \mod 10\). 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m - For \(a = 0\), the divisor is \(3^b \times 7^d\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m - We need to check the conditions for \(3^b \times 7^d \equiv 1 \mod 10\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m Analyzing the cases:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m - For \(b = 0, d = 0\), \(3^0 \times 7^0 = 1\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m - For \(b = 1, d = 1\), \(3^1 \times 7^1 = 3 \times 7 = 21\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m - For \(b = 4, d = 0\), \(3^4 \times 7^0 = 81 \times 1 = 81\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=391279)[0m 
